### What is the Merge Intervals problem?

The problem gives a list of intervals, each with a start and an end, and asks to combine all overlapping intervals into a minimal set of non-overlapping intervals that cover the same ranges. This is one of the most common interval problems in interviews.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class MergeIntervals {
    public int[][] merge(int[][] intervals) {
        if (intervals.length <= 1) return intervals;
        Arrays.sort(intervals, Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[0]));

        List<int[]> result = new ArrayList<>();
        int[] current = intervals[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < intervals.length; i++) {
            if (intervals[i][0] <= current[1]) {
                // Overlapping: extend current end
                current[1] = Math.max(current[1], intervals[i][1]);
            } else {
                // Gap: close current, start new
                result.add(current);
                current = intervals[i];
            }
        }
        result.add(current); // flush final
        return result.toArray(new int[0][]);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] intervals = {{1,3},{2,6},{8,10},{15,18}};
        int[][] merged = new MergeIntervals().merge(intervals);
        for (int[] m : merged) System.out.println("[" + m[0] + "," + m[1] + "]");
    }
}
```

```text
[1,6]
[8,10]
[15,18]
```

### Why is sorting by start the first step?

Sorting makes overlap detection local. Once intervals are ordered by start time, any interval that overlaps an earlier one must overlap the most recently merged interval, because everything is in start order. Without sorting, every interval would need to be compared against all others, giving O(n squared) time.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class WhySortByStart {
    // Unsorted: [8,10], [1,3], [2,6]
    // Sorted:   [1,3], [2,6], [8,10]
    //
    // After sorting, [2,6] only needs to compare with [1,3] (previous)
    // because if [2,6] overlaps anything, it overlaps the immediate predecessor.

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Sorting by start makes overlap detection O(n).");
        System.out.println("Without sorting: O(n^2) all-pairs comparison.");
    }
}
```

### Why take the maximum of the two ends?

When two intervals overlap, the merged interval's end is the maximum of the two ends, not simply the next interval's end. This matters when the next interval is fully contained within the current one. If you took the next interval's end, you would shrink the current interval.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MaxEndRule {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Current: [1, 10], Next: [2, 5]
        // Next is fully contained. End should stay 10, not shrink to 5.
        // Math.max(10, 5) = 10 (correct)

        System.out.println("[1,10] merge [2,5] => [1," + Math.max(10, 5) + "]");
        System.out.println("[1,3] merge [2,6] => [1," + Math.max(3, 6) + "]");
    }
}
```

```text
[1,10] merge [2,5] => [1,10]
[1,3] merge [2,6] => [1,6]
```

### How do you handle the final interval flush?

The loop closes the current interval only when a gap is found. If the loop ends without a gap (the last intervals overlap), the final merged interval is never pushed to the result. The fix is to always push the current interval after the loop ends.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FinalFlush {
    // WRONG: misses the last interval
    // for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    //     if (overlap) merge;
    //     else { result.add(current); current = next; }
    // }
    // return result; // BUG: current never added if last intervals overlap

    // CORRECT: add current after loop
    // result.add(current); // flushes the final merged interval

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Always add current after the loop to avoid off-by-one.");
    }
}
```

### Should touching intervals be merged?

Whether intervals that touch (one ends exactly where the next begins) should be merged is a domain decision. For maintenance windows at the bank, touching windows form a continuous outage, so use `<=` (less-than-or-equal). For booking slots where a customer leaves at 10:00 and the next arrives at 10:00, they are separate, so use `<` (strict less-than).

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TouchingPolicy {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // [1,5] and [5,8]
        // <= : merge into [1,8] (continuous block)
        // <  : separate [1,5] and [5,8]

        int end = 5, nextStart = 5;
        System.out.println("<= merge: " + (nextStart <= end)); // true
        System.out.println("<  merge: " + (nextStart < end));  // false
    }
}
```

```text
<= merge: true
<  merge: false
```

### How is this different from Insert Interval and Interval Intersection?

| Feature | Merge Intervals | Insert Interval | Interval Intersection |
|---|---|---|---|
| Input | unsorted list of intervals | sorted list + one new interval | two sorted lists |
| Goal | merge all overlapping | insert and merge | find overlapping portions |
| Sort needed | yes | no (already sorted) | no (already sorted) |
| Output | minimal merged list | minimal merged list | list of intersections |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class IntervalFamilyComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Merge: sort + sweep, merge overlapping.");
        System.out.println("Insert: sweep sorted list, merge around new interval.");
        System.out.println("Intersection: two-pointer, max-start to min-end.");
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a single interval (return it as-is), all intervals overlapping (merge into one), no overlapping intervals (return all), and intervals with zero length where start equals end.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Comparator;

public class MergeEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MergeIntervals solver = new MergeIntervals();

        // Single interval
        int[][] r = solver.merge(new int[][]{{1, 5}});
        System.out.println("Single: [" + r[0][0] + "," + r[0][1] + "]");

        // All overlapping
        r = solver.merge(new int[][]{{1,4},{2,5},{3,6}});
        System.out.println("All overlap: [" + r[0][0] + "," + r[0][1] + "]");

        // No overlapping
        r = solver.merge(new int[][]{{1,2},{5,6},{9,10}});
        System.out.println("No overlap: " + r.length + " intervals");
    }
}
```

```text
Single: [1,5]
All overlap: [1,6]
No overlap: 3 intervals
```

### What are common mistakes?

The two most common mistakes are forgetting to take the maximum of the ends (using the next end directly, which shrinks on containment) and forgetting to flush the final interval after the loop.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: uses next end directly, shrinks on containment
current[1] = intervals[i][1]; // BUG: should be Math.max
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: takes maximum to handle containment
current[1] = Math.max(current[1], intervals[i][1]);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows this is the base template for the entire merge-intervals family. Insert Interval, Interval Intersection, Meeting Rooms, and free-gap computation all build on the sort-and-sweep skeleton. They also know the time is O(n log n) from the sort with a linear merge pass, and the space is O(n) for the result. For timestamps they use `Instant.compareTo` and for money they use `BigDecimal.compareTo`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SeniorMergeNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior insights:");
        System.out.println("  1. Base template for all interval problems.");
        System.out.println("  2. O(n log n) sort + O(n) sweep.");
        System.out.println("  3. Max-end and final flush are the two correctness traps.");
        System.out.println("  4. Use Instant.compareTo for timestamps, BigDecimal for money.");
    }
}
```
